Part A — ERD Design

1. Draw a complete ERD. Include all entities, attributes, primary keys, foreign keys, relationship names, and cardinalities.

Resolve the many-to-many relationship between MEMBER and FITNESS_CLASS using CLASS_BOOKING.

Part B — Database Implementation

2. Create all tables using Oracle-compatible SQL. Choose suitable data types and define primary keys, foreign keys, and

appropriate constraints. Include constraints for required fields and sensible values where appropriate.

3. Insert realistic sample records into every table. Include members with no memberships, plans with no memberships,

trainers with no classes, classes with no bookings, memberships with no payments, and different payment statuses.

Part C — SQL Queries

Write one SQL query for each question. Return only the columns needed and use clear aliases where useful.

1. Membership details. Display each membership ID, member name, plan name, start date, and membership status.

Include only memberships with matching member and plan records. Use INNER JOIN.

2. Members and memberships. List every member and any membership they have. Members who have never had a

membership must still appear. Use LEFT JOIN.

3. Classes and bookings. List every fitness class and any booking made for it. Classes with no bookings must still

appear. Write the query using RIGHT JOIN by arranging the tables appropriately.

4. Trainers and classes. Display every trainer and the classes they teach, including trainers who have no classes. Use

LEFT JOIN.

